<section class="phero"><div class="phero-bg"><img src="/assets/img/photos/commercial-building.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/commercial-building-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/commercial-building.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="678" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.08"></div><div class="wrap phero-in"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><ol><li><a href="/">Home</a></li><li><a href="/appraisal-practice/">Services</a></li><li>Commercial &amp; HOA Appraisal</li></ol></nav><p class="tag">Appraisal service</p><h1>Commercial Insurance Appraisal for Ohio Buildings and Associations</h1><p class="lede">Larger properties bring bigger estimates, more buildings and more people at the table. We bring structure to commercial, association and house-of-worship claims that have stalled on the number.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn btn-red" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ico" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn btn-ghost-w" href="/contact-our-team/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="hero-facts"><div><dt>Property types</dt><dd>Commercial, multifamily, HOA</dd></div><div><dt>Roof systems</dt><dd>TPO, EPDM, mod-bit, metal</dd></div><div><dt>Decision makers</dt><dd>Owners, boards, managers</dd></div></dl></div></section><div class="wrap two-col"><article class="prose">
<h2>Who calls us for commercial work</h2>
<p>Commercial insurance appraisal in Ohio covers a wide range of owners. Some own a single retail strip in a Columbus suburb; others manage several apartment complexes across the Miami Valley. Condo and homeowner associations from Mentor to Mason hold master policies on dozens of buildings. Churches across the state carry policies on sanctuaries with steep slate roofs, stained glass and pipe organs that no standard estimating database prices well.</p>
<ul>
<li>Owners of office, retail, warehouse and light-industrial buildings</li>
<li>Apartment and multifamily owners and their property managers</li>
<li>HOA and condominium association boards on master policies</li>
<li>Churches, schools and nonprofit buildings</li>
<li>Mixed-use buildings in older downtowns such as Sandusky, Lima and Zanesville</li>
</ul>
<h2>Why these claims stall</h2>
<p>Commercial disputes rarely come down to one line. A hailstorm crossing northwest Ohio may hit twenty buildings in a complex, each with different roof ages and repair histories. A wind event can tear membrane off a flat roof and push water into tenant spaces below. The carrier may agree damage exists but scope only the worst slopes, or price low-slope work at residential rates. Boards and managers often face pressure from residents or tenants while the claim drags on.</p>
<p>Common sticking points include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Repair versus replacement on low-slope systems such as TPO, EPDM and modified bitumen</li>
<li>Whether undamaged buildings in a uniform complex must be addressed for consistent appearance</li>
<li>Code and ordinance coverage limits on older structures</li>
<li>Coinsurance and valuation clauses that affect the payout</li>
<li>Specialty items: bell towers, steeples, stained glass, commercial kitchens</li>
</ul>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Board members, read this</b>An association board usually has to authorize an appraisal demand and the appraiser’s engagement. Put the decision in your minutes and appoint one point of contact for the claim. Fewer voices means faster decisions once the panel starts working.</div>
<h2>Tenants, residents and business income</h2>
<p>When a commercial building or apartment complex is damaged, the loss keeps growing while repairs wait. Tenants may withhold rent, residents may need temporary housing and a business may lose income for weeks. Many commercial policies include business income or rental value coverage, often with a waiting period and a limited period of restoration. Appraisal can usually address the amount of these losses when the policy’s appraisal clause reaches them, but the documentation burden is heavier: leases, rent rolls, profit-and-loss statements and records of extra expense. We coordinate with your accountant or property manager so the building scope and the income figures tell the same story about how long the repair should take.</p>
<h2>How we organize a multi-building file</h2>
<p>We build a property-by-property scope with a summary sheet the board or owner can follow. Each building gets its own roof diagram, elevation photos and pricing, so the carrier’s appraiser can review one structure at a time. Where it helps, the panel can agree to resolve buildings in groups instead of waiting for the full file. Our <a href="/appraisal-workbench/roof-area-pitch-calculator/">roof area and pitch calculator</a> is handy for a first look at sloped buildings in a complex.</p>
<h2>Deductibles and holdbacks on larger policies</h2>
<p>Commercial and association policies may carry percentage wind/hail deductibles, per-building deductibles or blanket limits. Before appraisal starts, we model how the award will flow through those terms so there are no surprises when the check arrives. The <a href="/appraisal-workbench/percentage-deductible-calculator/">percentage deductible calculator</a> gives a quick illustrative view.</p>
<h2>Related services</h2>
<p>If the dispute is mainly about the roofing scope, see our <a href="/appraisal-practice/hail-roof-appraisal/">hail and roof appraisal</a> page. For a quick check on whether appraisal is worth pursuing, an <a href="/appraisal-practice/estimate-audit/">estimate audit</a> is a sensible first step.</p>
<h2>Questions about commercial &amp; hoa appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Can an HOA board invoke appraisal on the master policy?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Generally the named insured on the master policy, which is usually the association, makes the demand. Your declaration and bylaws describe who can act for the association, so confirm the board has authority and record the vote. An Ohio attorney can advise on governance questions.</p></div></details><details><summary>Do churches use the same appraisal process as businesses?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Most church property policies contain an appraisal clause similar to other commercial forms. The difference is usually in pricing specialty features such as slate, stained glass and steeples, which need careful documentation and specialist input.</p></div></details><details><summary>Can one appraisal cover multiple buildings in a complex?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It often can, if they are on the same claim and policy. The appraisers and umpire can also agree to resolve buildings in batches, which keeps progress moving on large files.</p></div></details><details><summary>How does coinsurance affect a commercial appraisal award?</summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Appraisal sets the amount of loss, but a coinsurance clause can reduce what the policy pays if the building was underinsured. Review the limits and valuation terms with your agent or an attorney before relying on the full award figure.</p></div></details></div>
